    "text": "Well, hello everybody. It's been a couple days. I'm just going to come on here for a second and talk to you guys. I want to talk about music deals as we've uh been talking about them a lot the last two months. I feel like I actually was going to post another video today or tomorrow, but there was news today that Blastoff uh one of the most, if not the most popular music deal company got shut down. I don't fully know if that's the right terminology to use. Um they could just be on pause potentially. I don't know. I never want to count anything out completely, but basically all of their music was removed. And if you go to their uh artist page where they posted all of their music, you'll see there's no content here, which is what has been happening to all of these other music deals. So, Playlist, Any Mind, uh there was a few more too. AATIC, I think. So, I'm not going to go super deep into like the reasoning for this. Obviously, the only important reasoning that matters is that YouTube hates music do companies at this point. That's the only thing you can gather from this. YouTube doesn't really want these companies around anymore. Now, maybe it's just because a lot of them were breaking the policies. Maybe, you know what? Hopefully, for everybody's sake, all of these music deal companies come back. You know what I mean? I don't know the future of that, right? In a few days, Blastoff could come back. In a few days, Play come back. I have no clue. Okay? I can't predict the future. All I know is that there's clearly a trend that YouTube is shutting down all of these uh big companies. So, for the foreseeable future, they are probably cooked. But I will keep you guys updated. I mean, if something happens. So, let's talk about the future of music deals. So, I guess there's a few important things. So, uh first and foremost, the one the music deal that I've been using in the meantime is Duna. So, they are still up right now. They're safe. Like, they haven't gotten notified by YouTube or anything, and none of their music has been unlisted. However, I talked to the owner. They are closing all the applications, and they're not inviting in new people at this moment. Um, which is probably smart because YouTube is cracking down so hard on these companies. I don't know if that will help them stay around long term. Like, if like if YouTube's cracking down on the biggest companies, then who knows the future of all the smallest ones. So, in the meantime, I'm still going to be using their music in my shorts, but um who knows the future of that. I'll keep you guys updated with that. The second thing is Shortsrev hopefully will be releasing soon within the next few months. I don't know exactly when, but hopefully soon. Shortsrev is just a new music deal and hopefully they'll have a few things in place to prevent against this happening because as far as I understand, a lot of these big companies don't even have a CMS, like a content management system with YouTube. But I do think that Shortsrev is going to have more connections just because of the people who are actually launching it are very competent in my opinion. But, you know, they haven't even launched yet, so we don't know what's going on with that. Third, and most importantly, my next step, as far as me personally, my next step is probably going to be distributing my own music. Um, so there's a few different distributors you can use. The one I'll probably be using is Amuse, so I can distribute my own music. You can't just do AI with this, by the way. So, don't think about that. You'll have to hire actual producers um to make beats that you can then use. Um, but you'll keep more of the revenue. Now, I did try that before with uh this video that I posted, but I think the reason that my RPM was so low when I tested it with this video is because people use the song in a bunch of viral shorts that were like under 35 seconds. And if you know anything about music deals, the RPM sucks if i