on. Right? Which video would you rather watch? And which video do you think most people want to watch? A ranking video or a Zack Dilms video, right? This guy who does the 3D animation stuff. I'm sure you guys have seen his videos. His content is insanely viral, right? This question is actually important because you need to be thinking about why you Oh my god. why you find things interesting. Why do you find things compelling, interesting, or engaging? Because if you want to make something that's compelling, engaging, and interesting, you need to understand what actually goes into that. And that's why your biggest advantage in this game is learning the game. Study people, consume, right? It's the same concept with like professional gaming, right? If you want to get really good at a game, one of the things that you should be doing is watching how the pro players, the people you want to emulate actually do the game, how they actually play the game. I mean, right? I usually hate when people are like egregious consumers, but with social media like this, it is different. Consume, consume, consume as much as you can because you're trying to emulate. I know we say copy and like make it better a lot, but elaborating on that, what you're really trying to do is that you should be trying to emulate a channel or a person who's successful because you want to recreate their success, right? That's the name of the game. We all want to be successful. Now, there's some slight variance, but generally speaking, most people would want to watch a video like from Zack Dilms more than just a regular generalized ranking video. And that's for many reasons, but a few are just that the content, number one, is not cookie cutter, right? like it's an animation style that people can recreate if they know how to do it. But again, take the ranking niche for example. There's literally like a format that you use for the video, right? So, this is just something that is you have a template for basically.