s. Know which way you're going to attack something. If you're on a battlefield, you're not just going to send your soldiers in rushing in to to war without a plan. You're going to say, \"Oh, we want to be on this this hill or we want to be in this trench or we want to hold the upper ground.\" That's just the way it goes. You got to have an angle for attack to be successful. That's called strategy. Without a strategy, without an angle, you're throwing darts at a board you cannot see. Now again with YouTube automation, what you're going to find is that a lot of these niches or things that you are going to embark on are going to be is just going to be content that is simply not original or it's going to have low originality. And this is truly truly the crux with YouTube automation and this dream that people sell you, right? Essentially that you can outsource the work and it'll be passive because somebody else will be doing the work and you'll just be collecting and raking in the cash. Now, of course, it's possible, right? Um it happens a lot for people, right? And I say a lot, I don't mean statistically common. I just mean in the world people can do it. But for most people, you're not just going to be able to outsource everything and essentially automate your YouTube channel. Now, why is this? It's because largely people are successful on YouTube because they built up a skill set. So again, back to this example, right? This animation style is something that they do. This is what they're doing the best. Zact Films, like I showed you earlier, that 3D animation is something that he's known for. It's a skill set they can do that they have acquired. In the case of Zacti Films, of course, it is a great example of what can happen and to some extent that content for him is automated because he's not the one animating all this stuff. But you guys got to keep in mind this is somebody who has 1.8K videos. This is extremely late game stuff, right? This guy is spending hundreds of 