e period where I made all of it. So, it just goes to show you how v uh volatile shorts can be. Okay. Um, I know I'm kind of just jumping into this right now without much of an explanation, but basically the point I want to make with this video is um, pretty obvious if you guys hopefully get what I mean, which is just that shorts can be very volatile. Now, now by themselves, these channels generate a decent amount of revenue, right? And I'm working on getting a couple others monetized. I just got two more monetized that'll bring me in a small amount of money. And I'm working on getting more monetized, but I'm kind of reaching that point where it's like I got to start outsourcing some of this stuff or else I won't be able to do everything, which is part of the reason why I haven't been uploading that much on this channel. Right? When you take a look at that graph that was completely flatlined for all of September that whole time, that's me trying to desperately build up other channels as well, because I'm like, \"Holy, I'm getting cooked by YouTube.\" And so that's and that's what I was kind of talking about in this video where I said I hadn't been super active on this channel and I want to increase my upload frequency. But first and foremost, since I'm not making any money really from this channel, I got to focus on actually making income, right? You know, I think that's fair. But a lot of these other guys, they have courses or 101 ones, and that's actually where bulk of their income stability comes from. And so that's what I want to talk about today. But because I'm not doing that on this channel, I don't have that stability. Okay? The only thing I had going for me was a bit of affiliate revenue coming from the music deal uh playlist, but that's on pause right now. If you're still confused about that, you can watch this video, this update video, if you have not watched that already because I talk about playlist and like important dates you need to know for the future. But anyways, the point I'm trying to make, if you want stable income, sell a course, do one-on- ones, you know, become one of these gurus and uh don't do what I do cuz trust me, this is not lucrative and you just get a bunch of annoying kids who are immature spamming you. That's actually what I get. I don't get money. So let's talk about this a little bit. So YouTube shorts as a business model is very unstable. Now I do think that with the right niche with the right ideas basically if you're in an evergreen niche I do think that I do think that it can be something that generates you consistent money but um consistent money and stable money are two very different things I guess. So my revenue kind of varies wildly from one month to the next and it just depends on if I'm getting screwed by the YouTube algorithm, if I have bad videos that month or any different number of factors, right? So it can be very unstable. Still generating uh decent revenue but unstable nonetheless. So the the question then becomes how do we make YouTube shorts more stable? How do we hedge against this instability? So to me the answer seems pretty simple. So for starters, you hedge against this by running multiple channels. So for example, I have well let's just say I have two channels running, right? And I want to make 5,000 a month. This means that I'm going to need each channel to generate 2.5, right? Well, if I have five channels running, then I only need a th000 each channel, which $1,000 for each channel is really not that much work. If you start to outsource videos once you get monetized, then you can actually continue to grow this. But that's largely where I have problems actually finding people because ju just broadly speaking, right, if somebody's really good at what they do, they're not going to be letting you pay them five bucks to let you rake in like thousands and thousands of dollars. They're just going to be making the videos themselves. Okay? That's just how it goes. That's 