to try to post for the first time after that. If it's stuck in the zero of due jail, try warming it up for a few more days and then try posting again. The next thing is that you're going to go to YouTube Studio on the account you're wanting to upload on. Make sure this is a laptop or computer and you're going to click the settings button in the bottom left corner and then you're going to select channel. I usually select the country, so just do United States. But then you're also going to go to feature eligibility here. And what you're going to do is you're going to make sure each of these steps are completed and then you're going to save the changes. Now, don't just complete these uh verification steps and then instantly start posting. Make sure these are completed first thing and for a few days before you upload. They'll usually want you to do a phone number verification and then like a video verification with your face in it. Um and you can just do that with your phone. Don't worry, nobody's going to see your goofy a face. It's all automated and done by robots. Now, you can only verify uh two channels per year with the same phone number. So, if you've already done that, you should be able to get a free phone number by using Google Voice, or you can use a friend or a family member's phone number and have them give you the verification code. Just make sure it's not somebody who you'd ever have a fallout with. So, like your mom or dad would be good options if you have a non-abusive family. If you've already done these things and tried with multiple accounts, check to see if they're all under the same email. And you can either make a new email or try a different email account. Some email accounts are just legitimately boned. I have no idea why, but like no matter what you do, they will just not work. Like I said, I know a lot of this is going to just be frustrating troubleshooting, but if you can find a good email address that works for you, you can usually create a few different channels. And with a proper warm-up, sometimes any account on that email will get pushed, which will completely avoid zero view jail. For example, all of my successful accounts really come from like one email, and I have like five, bro. Now, when you start to upload, if you're just starting to upload, I would only upload once per day. On my first channel, if I ever uploaded within the same like 10-hour period, the second video just wouldn't get pushed. Even today, I mostly only do one short per day on all of my channels. Although, once your channel blows up, most of the time you can upload more. On my main channel, until I got like 10,000 subs, I swear every once in a while, my videos would just not get pushed. So, it would be like 15K views, 15K views, 15K views, 15K views, 100 views, 50 views, back to 15K views. It was like the dumbest thing ever. In most cases, if the video isn't getting pushed to the shorts feed after 1 hour and you already have other videos getting pushed, I personally unlist the video. Now, some people say it works for them when they private it. But the most important thing is that you just do not delete it. Only unlist it or private it. And just a side note here, the only reason that I only wait an hour is just because the channel already has been pushed before. If it's a new channel, wait at least 24 to 48 hours to see if you're really stuck in zero view jail. cuz sometimes the algorithm is just weird and it's like these videos will get pushed in like a day or two. But anyways, you unlist it or private it, then wait a day and upload it again with different captions, a different title, and also when you upload it, upload it on your phone in YouTube, and you're going to cut off like 0.1 or 2 seconds at the end of the video. So, this will change the metadata of the video so YouTube doesn't think you're spamming the same video. And this is super important. If you're going to upload the same video that got zero views to a different c