oney Mind Unfiltered. I'm sure most of you guys probably know him. On the channel that's stuck in zero view jails, upload a long form video with your face in it just talking about something random. Leave it up for a day or two, then unlist that video and try to post shorts again. Anecdotally, something you guys might find interesting is that most of my successful channels that got pushed, I actually was uploading long form before I repurposed those channels. And the long form videos I uploaded before didn't even have my face in them. It was just me playing games or something. If you don't use an account for like 5 years, I've never had success posting on an account like that, even if it had previous activity and was aged. Now, I'm not saying it can't work for you, but I think if you're extremely inactive on a YouTube channel for years or even months, and then you come back and start suddenly uploading videos, even though the account is aged, it's never really worked for me. Definitely. Definitely for those super old channels you haven't used in years, I would say you still need to warm them up. Don't think just because it's an aged account, it'll work out. Now, copyright strikes, not copyright claims, but copyright strikes, they would show up around here in your dashboard. They have always killed any reach I had until they were either resolved or until exactly 2 weeks from the strike being issued. In my personal experience, literally the one time I had a copyright strike, it was at like 3 p.m. and I lost all of my views right then and there. Dropped off a cliff. And I swear to God, like 14 days later at exactly 3:00 p.m., it's like my view faucet just turned back on. It was insane. Another thing, scheduling shorts has never pushed it to the algorithm for me. I don't know why. I know other people do it and it works fine for them. It'll be different for everybody, but it always put me in zero view jail and not getting pushed to the shorts feed every single time. I would upload normally if you can. Maybe that's just a me thing. Now, make sure when you upload a video, you select no, this content is not made for kids, unless it is specifically marketed for like toddlers. And also, you want to check no, this video does not contain altered content, unless you're literally posting AI videos, in which case you do want to hit that. Also, when you upload a video or just on your channel, in the settings, go to category and change this to whatever category you are posting in. If it's like fitness, for example, it's just people and blogs. If it's gaming, it's a gaming right here. You're going to want to be careful about drastically and randomly switching up the type of content you're posting. Sometimes this can confuse the algorithm. You can post different videos. Like, don't be scared, but if you change your channel from like military videos to like celebrities, the algorithm might be confused for a bit. You're also going to want to make sure that the content you're posting isn't just re-uploads of other people's videos. Do not just go on to Tik Tok, download a video like this. You want to make sure you're editing the videos or tweaking them a bit. If the videos are just ripped and re-uploaded, nine times out of 10, they won't get pushed. You need to add something new. YouTube especially shadowbans channels that do this. That's why, for like example, there are channels that literally just re-upload a video from FitFix and they usually get shadowbanned even though the original video has like millions of views. Because even though the video is highquality and can get a lot of views when FitFix posted it, YouTube detects you're using unoriginal content and flags that video and your channel. So then when we go to his channel, notice how pretty much nothing is getting pushed. If you've been uploading for a while and got pushed and then suddenly you're stuck in zero view jail, it could be a few things. Number one, start uploading every other day instead of once or twice every day. Number two, you're going to try to upload from a different device. Like if you usually upload from your phone, try uploading from your computer and vice versa. Number three, if you're uploading on your computer, there should be an option that says send this video first to your subscribers or something. And you're going to want to uncheck that box. It should be in like the advanced settings when you're uploading a video. So, just uncheck show your subscribers first and try to upload it again. Another thing you can do is test out whether the shorts hashtag in your title has any effect. So, instead of the title just being like this guy did something crazy, it'll be this guy did something crazy # shorts. Adding on to that, you can also try to upload from within the YouTube Studio app on your phone instead of from the YouTube app. The time you upload doesn't really matter that much, but just try to upload around the same time every time you post. For example, if you're posting every day around 6:00 p.m., you can post at like 7:00 p.m. or 5:30 p.m. But I wouldn't just randomly post at 8:00 a.m. one day. Also, don't use the same title on shorts that you post every day. YouTube might mark it as spam. So, for example, so like for example, for every video, don't make the title like don't click the sound or something. It can work for some people, but if you're getting zero views and doing that, cut it out, bro. If you've had a video up for a few days with zero views, before you unlist it, you could always try to change the title, add shorts in the title, like I said, change the description a little bit. I have had a video that was stuck on zero views before I changed the title, and immediately it started getting views. Now, if you've tried everything and have been consistently uploading once per day or once every other day for two weeks while using the account and interacting with other people's content and none of the videos have been push