entary channels exist, why they're a thing. Also, why short form commentary channels exist, that's just the way it is. YouTube has always been a platform where you can comment over other people's videos, right? So, as long as you're taking that content and you're actually transforming it to a substantial degree, then I don't think that anybody will have this problem, right? again. So, the people who should be worried are the people who are mass- prodducing repetitious content like YouTube says. Now, obviously, like I said, this is pretty vague. It's pretty annoying that YouTube did this right now. In complete transparency, I'm not omnipotent. I'm not God. Okay? I can't tell you what YouTube is going to do, especially because they'll probably have to come out in a few days or maybe on July 15th when this rule change actually gets implemented and lay out more of what they mean because right now it's incredibly unspecific. So, we'll see what they end up doing or saying, and if they make any updates, hey, I'll be here. I'll make a video about it. Now, let's talk about the second reason why this is probably not going to result in this mass apocalypse of every single faceless YouTube shorts channel. And that's because simply YouTube is a company. Okay, again, taking FitFix, this guy has generated 9 billion views for YouTube. Okay, do you guys have any idea how much ad revenue YouTube has made off this guy? Do you guys know how much money companies will pay, huge corporations will pay to be in front of 9 billion views on YouTube if they nuke people like this? If they just demonetized everybody who did this, right? Cuz this is just one guy, right? Like think about this guy, one of the OGs of uh YouTube shorts, Dylan Anderson, and he's just doing and he's just doing commentary shorts as well, right? Faceless. This guy has gotten 34 billion views for YouTube and for the companies that pour ad revenue into it. And and even moreover, YouTube is trying to compete with Tik Tok. I