  "transcript": {
    "text": "The most important news of the week is that I invested in the fourth YouTube channel this year. This one in particular is very special. I cannot reveal the niche right now, but I'm very excited to see how far this channel can go. We truly have just one competitor in a niche and uh this guy is getting 13 million views on average. So, we will be the first ones to try and post 30 to 40 videos a month in that niche. And uh half a billion views is the potential that I'm looking to get on this particular channel. And of course, I'll update you weekly on how we scale it from this moment on. A portfolio of our YouTube shorts channels made $10,000 last week. It wasn't the best week I've seen. I remember hitting $20,000 weeks with just one of these channels. But in YouTube business, it's uh always ups and downs. and then apps again. Taking into account that one of these channels was falsely terminated and then reinstated uh last week, I'm pretty happy with a 5 figure result this week. And because we've just added a fourth channel to this portfolio, soon we'll see way more than 20 and 30k weeks. Now, as usual, this news digest will have a lot of sauce, but this one is very special. First, I'll show you the most important news of the week. And uh there are a few that are really gamechanging for uh creators, especially faceless creators. Then I'll show you the top performing formats that you can start using right now, no matter the niche. And then at the end, my team prepared a list of seven untapped niches that are blowing up this week. So you can simply copy one of them and get started with Faceless YouTube automation. So without further ado, let's get started. YouTube Shorts has finally launched this massive update on the official website. We are enabling Shorts creators to link the brand's websites to their branded content. This is absolutely huge because when I, a faceless creator with over 1.5 million followers and over four billion views, tried reaching out to brands to sponsor my content, I got laughed at. Nobody wanted to give me a single penny for a sponsored short. That is because you couldn't add a link directly to uh the video, meaning brands couldn't track the effectiveness of a campaign. And I was so jealous looking at the lawn form creators who are charging 5 to 10 grand for a 30 secondond sponsored integration in a video that would barely get more than 100k views while I was sitting on videos getting hundreds of millions of views. But this is finally going to change. Uh this is how the uh sponsored post is going to uh look like. And the channels like Art of War, for example, uh will finally be able to charge $3 to $5,000 for a simple YouTube shirt. Sora will now be able to AI generate videos based on characters from Disney, Marvel, Pixar, and Star Wars. So if you ever tried to uh generate Disney characters with charity, you know that it gives you absolutely nothing because Disney wanted to protect its IP. However, last week Disney invested a billion dollars into open AI and now Sora and I guess Charpt as well will be able to generate pictures and videos of our favorite characters. I can only imagine how many short-term opportunities it will open for faceless YouTube creators. Uh but uh be careful because uh this is still uh using someone else's IP to promote your stuff. Another massive YouTube news this week. YouTube has finally launched Ask Studio. This is essentially an AI inside your YouTube dashboard uh designed to consult you on your YouTube video performance. For example, you can ask why didn't my last video perform? Or for example, what video brought in the most subscribers? To me, it sounds useful as a navigation tool because uh YouTube analytics might be confusing. It looks like a bunch of uh buttons here and there like you're trying to launch a rocket, especially if you're a beginner. But uh now you can just ask AI whatever you want to know about your audience and uh it will simply guide you uh through it. And of course, I wanted to test it out. But uh when I opened my channel dashboard, I learned that uh this thing is only available in US, Canada, and India. So well, Indians are enjoying YouTube's new AI. We creators from Europe are going to do the analytics the old way. Fortune breaking news. YouTube launches option for US creators to receive stable coin payouts through PayPal. Now, when choosing a payment option in your AdSense, you can not only choose a bank account, but also a payout in stable coins through PayPal. Before, it was an extra benefit that MCN were providing you with, but now YouTube is doing the exact same thing. I bet more platforms will follow Tik Tok, Facebook, and others. And soon, every creator on Earth will withdraw money in crypto. Another YouTube update this week. You can now reply to your comments on YouTube with a voice note. Neil Moan, the CEO of YouTube, tweeted it saying, \"YouTube teams continue to ship relentlessly for our creators.\" Well, Neil, instead of launching yet another useless update, maybe try fixing your [ __ ] AI dock, the one that's mindlessly bulldozing the very creators you ship relentlessly for. YouTube Create has officially launched in the US. Essentially, it's a free mobile editing app made by YouTube. Think of it like Cap Cut, but uh everything is free. I don't know who's going to use it, but now you know it exists. All right. Now, I'm going to give you a few viral formats that been blowing up on YouTube uh this week. So, no matter the niche, you can adapt it and somehow use it for uh your channel because that's the stuff that works on YouTube. Now, this stupid video compilation got 820k views and made this creator $3,000. And I'm not the one to discuss compilation niches. As you know, I'm into branded long-term channels. But what I find interesting is the format and the thumbnail of this video because this was literally the first long form video they posted on the channel after a couple of unsuccessful shorts and it exploded to almost a million views in 2 weeks. So, what's up with the format for this video? They used Roblox versus real life in a whiteboard sticker style format. And there are so many good niches where you can adopt the same exact style. It reminds me of the explained with a duck channel, but basically any semi-educational channel can now adopt this style as it's something that blows up on YouTube right now. Mr. Bis has changed the thumbnail for one versus 100K firework and the views quintipled. I had to learn a new word to say this, but uh there's a huge lesson in why he changed it. The after thumbnail is a typical comparison thumbnail like the one we discussed with the Roblox Brain Rod. When you split your screen into two or even three sections, you instantly give the viewer more context on what's going to happen next. And that's why it drives way more views. A very interesting experiment that I saw on Twitter this week. This guy, Jack Bryant, posted a video with two similar thumbnails, but different titles using a new AB test feature we discussed last week. And while most people would expect that titles don't really matter as long as the thumbnail is the same, one of them actually got 77% watchtime share, which is almost double of what the other one got. This goes to show that viewers decide whether to watch the video or not based on the combination of thumbnail and title, not just on one specific thing. And one more thing I wanted to give you in the format section before I show you the best faceless YouTube niches we found is a billion views triangle. You see, let me tell you a secret that no one in the faceless YouTube space will ever tell you about making content. Most people think a good hook is what makes a video go viral. Some say it's a good story, good analogy, example, etc. But when I think about all of the YouTube shorts and even long form videos that we posted in the past year, the ones that have performed the best, it wasn't because the de