o, when you offer them 1.2k a month, 1.5k a month, they're amazed. Like, they're making like twice as their average salary in the country. I would hire the same like animator from US, it's like 6K minimum. >> And here is where I want you to pay close attention. >> The real genius behind it was not the the niche or the topics or the videos themselves. It was actually the hiring strategy because the only reason why this strategy was profitable is because our expenses were so low compared to all our other competitors. Like the films, he does 3 billion views a month and he's paying $1,000 per short. Like the same same quality like 1,000 bucks. And we are now averaging like 150, 160. So we pay what what's that? Like five five times less for almost the same quality. >> So while every other creator was obsessed with views, Tim was focusing on reducing costs without sacrificing the quality of his content. And once you can do that, you let the maths take care of themselves. Kind of. >> We four-Xed the amount of content because I basically four-Xed the team. So we hired like five animators at the same time and with that we started producing 60 videos and that's that's how the whole jump happened. >> And you don't have to imagine what that does to the revenue of a channel. Because Tim showed us. And it only took 60 days. >> We basically went from $7,000 in April to $50,000 in in June in just like 60 days. >> And it's not just about the huge increase in revenue that the channel was making. Tim also pulled this off without chaining himself to the computer 24/7. >> I was planning the channel as a business not as a creative side hustle. Once they were built, I didn't spend more than 10 hours a week managing the channel. >> 10 hours a week. Have you got creator envy? Because I sure do. But let's move on to the question you actually came here for. How do you find a market or a niche that's both lucrative and wide open for you to enter? >> Your YouTube like faceless YouTube niche is 