n of this, someone else would already have a six-year head start on you. That's what makes a commitment series so sharable. People aren't sharing the video. They're sharing the premise. There's a guy beating every N64 game. There's a channel that covered World War II in real time for 6 years. The commitment is worth sharing. And that is the purple cow. The simplest type though is when the creator themselves is the purple cow. Real credentials so unexpected that the bio itself is the hook. Philip Chase is an English professor with a PhD in medieval literature. Decades studying old English, old Norse, Baolf. But his channel's big break wasn't talking about his specific expertise, per se. English professor's thoughts on One Piece. That's the whole pitch. He's working his way through an insanely popular manga, arc by arc, with the analytical tools of someone who spent his career in the texts that One Piece was drawn from. 54,000 subscribers, and the share text writes itself. Fans of the story are clicking on that link before they even finish reading the text. And then there's Gus, a student who just published a video called Introducing Kendrick Lamar to an Italian Poet. a video with almost 400,000 views on a channel with under 4,000 subscribers in just 8 days. He sat his Italian poetry professor down in front of Kendrick's lyrics and asked him to analyze them like they were any literature. The professor compared Kendrick's rhetorical structures to Dante and Petra. Without any context, you look at the title and you immediately want to see what happens. Improbable source works best when the credentials in question aren't immediately like a good fit at first glance, but the more you think about them, the more it kind of makes sense. A medieval literature professor isn't exactly who you would think about when you consider somebody reviewing One Piece. But One Piece is built on the same mythological and heroic traditions he spent his career studying. An Italian poetry professor isn't who you'd expect to analyze Kendrick. But Kendrick also just won a Pulitzer. The connection was always there. And the improbable source was the purple cow that made it so sharable. However, a purple cow isn't free. And there are two ways they can fail you and do way more damage than that initial decision to be different ever would. The first one is copycats. Once your purple cow works, other people will copy it. And the more successful your purple cow is, the faster those copycats will come aboard. Remember the ASMR gun sounds channel from before? Well, yeah, originally that video was a purple cow, but once everyone came aboard and did their version of it, it was no longer remarkable. And right now, the same thing is happening to that improbable source format we just talked about. The Italian Professor Kendrick video works because nobody else has that exact framing of a literature professor analyzing grap. But the broader idea of an older or unexpected person reacting to rap is one of the more copied formats on YouTube. The front room is a dad and son channel where the dad reacts to modern hip hop. 360,000 subscribers. It worked really well when they were one of the first channels doing it. Now there are dozens of dad reacts to rap channels out there and none of them are growing the way the front room originally did. That purple cow has just decayed into a saturated micro niche. The difference between the Italian Professor Kendrick video and a generic dad reacts to rap channel is the specificity of the improbable source. Italian professor comparing Kendrick to Dante is much harder to copy. Older person reacts to rap can be copied a thousand times before lunch. This is why the commitment premise is the most defensible type of purple cow. Nobody can just copy four years of work overnight. But the other three types of purple cow have to work a lot harder to stay remarkable. Anyway, the second reason a purple cow strategy can fail you is because novelty has a 