ken down as just the sum of thousands of these sine waves at different frequencies and times. He's giving a collegiate-level audio engineering course, but disguising it as a video about Zelda. And here's what separates this channel from the others we've looked at. Sierra Laduke just built one great horse, but Marshall McGee made the horse the entire architecture of his career. He's never once titled a video around the thing he's actually discussing. Instead, he packages for the fandom that he wants to speak to. The whole channel is one continuous Trojan horse and inside is an audio engineering degree. So, what's the underlying pattern here? Well, you need two things. First is the topic that you're going to discuss, the the skill, the idea that you care about and that goes inside the box. Second, what is it that already has an audience? And I don't mean your audience, I mean what has an established audience around it. It can be a sport, a game, a feeling that millions of people carry around inside, a topic that people are already invested in, one they're already clicking on. Your job is to find where those two things genuinely connect. You want to make lo-fi music, but unfortunately, nobody searches for unknown artists. So, you scored every scene from The Office using only lo-fi beats. You want to talk about personal finance, and you happen to love reality TV. So, every real housewife ranked by how fast they'd go broke. Your topic isn't necessarily the problem. You don't want to change what you're trying to deliver. You just need to make the door that people walk through appealing, so they can get to it. But even when you build a great horse, and the packaging is right, and the content is strong, and the value is genuinely there, sometimes your video still doesn't get views. And you might think to yourself, \"Well, the algorithm is clearly having a bad day.\" But sometimes, that's not the case. It's actually the audience's fault. In this video, we talk about the vari